 "article": {
  "title": "Paper: The Invention That Quietly Changed the World",
  "topic": "人文历史",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 318,
  "paras": [
   "Long before screens glowed in every pocket, ideas travelled on thin sheets of beaten fibre. Paper was invented in China around the first century, and tradition credits a court official named Cai Lun with improving the craft in 105 AD. By soaking bark, rags and fishnets, then pressing the pulp flat and drying it in the sun, his workshops produced a light, cheap surface for writing. It was far easier to carry than heavy bamboo strips or precious silk, and a single sheet could hold a short letter, a poem or a bill of sale.",
   "The knowledge did not stay at home. It spread east to Korea and Japan, and westward along trade routes, reaching the Islamic world by the eighth century. There, in cities like Baghdad and Samarkand, paper mills multiplied, books became affordable, and scholars copied and compared texts on a scale the ancient world had never seen. When the craft finally entered Europe, through Spain and later Italy, it helped loosen the grip of parchment, which had kept reading a luxury for the wealthy few.",
   "Then came the printing press in the fifteenth century, and the two technologies together changed everything. Cheap paper plus movable type meant that a farmer's son could learn to read, that a scientist's notes could travel across borders, and that ordinary people could argue about politics in print. Literacy climbed, schools spread, and the modern public sphere slowly took shape on a foundation of humble wood pulp. Printers, rather than princes, became the new gatekeepers of fresh ideas.",
   "Today we scroll instead of turning pages, and some predict the end of paper. Yet paper has not vanished; it survives in letters, in archives, and in the quiet pleasure of holding a book. Its real lesson may be this: the technologies that shape history are rarely the loudest. A simple sheet, cheap and unremarkable, carried the words that built our world."
